<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sankin-kōtai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sankin-kotai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sankin-kōtai, or alternate attendance, was a system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> requiring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> to divide their time between their domains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s capital at Edo, leaving their families in Edo as hostages. The costly journeys and households drained the lords' resources and limited their ability to rebel. The system also stimulated roads, towns and commerce across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sankin-kotai.jpg" alt="Sankin-kōtai" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: a former retainer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Seiseiroku_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sankin-kōtai, meaning alternate attendance, was a policy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> for most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, created to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and keep them from trying to overthrow the regime. Earlier practices prepared the way. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> had required his lords to keep their wives and heirs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> or nearby as hostages. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> and the founding of the Tokugawa shogunate the practice continued at Edo as a custom. It became compulsory for the tozama daimyo in 1635 and for the fudai daimyo from 1642. Apart from an eight-year interval under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a>, the law stayed in force until 1862.</p><p>Kamakura-period gokenin had owed guard duty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> and attendance at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s court, and sons of local officials sometimes served there as hostages. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumazawa-banzan">Kumazawa Banzan</a> described detailed rules of alternate attendance in the Kamakura shogunate, which modern scholars cannot confirm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In general each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of a domain moved between Edo and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, typically spending alternate years in each, while his wife and heir remained in Edo as hostages. Some fudai daimyo near Edo alternated every six months, and illness or other grave circumstances brought temporary exceptions. In principle the system was a military service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, and each daimyo had to furnish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> in proportion to the kokudaka assessment of his domain, who accompanied him on the journeys.</p><p>Hundreds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> entered or left Edo each year, so processions were almost daily sights in the capital. They followed the main routes, the kaido, and stayed at special lodgings called honjin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Maintaining lavish residences in both places and paying for the journeys cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> dearly. Expenses of the system regularly made up 70 to 80 percent of their yearly spending. Because their income depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, which was limited by what a domain could produce, incomes failed to keep up and the daimyo fell into debt. Economic programmes, efforts to raise production and special taxes gave no lasting solution. By the Genroku period practically every daimyo was deep in debt, and even the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a> of Kaga, the wealthiest, ran into difficulties.</p><p>The frequent travel also encouraged road building and the construction of inns along the routes, which stimulated economic activity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> later had a similar practice, requiring nobles to spend six months of each year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sankin-kōtai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Tokugawa kin (shinpan), hereditary vassals (fudai) or 'outside' lords (tozama); the Buke Shohatto regulations of 1615; and the <b>sankin-kotai</b> system of alternate attendance, formalised by the third shogun Iemitsu in 1635, which required daimyo to spend alternate years in Edo and to leave their wives and heirs there…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
